==============================================================================
                          S C H E M A F R E E
        a surveyor's field book for label-free cross-cohort federation
==============================================================================

  Four laboratories on three continents look at cervical cytology through
  four incompatible grading languages. SchemaFree treats each as a survey
  party occupying its own station: every party measures the same terrain
  (cell morphology) with its own instrument, and the network is adjusted
  into one consistent map (a shared encoder) WITHOUT any party handing over
  its raw field notes (images) or agreeing on a common legend (label
  taxonomy). Naming the cells is left to each party, afterwards, in its own
  language, with the few labels it has.

  This book records how the federation is laid out, occupied, adjusted, and
  checked.


STATION 0 — WHAT IS IN THE FIELD BOOK
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  src/schemafree/
    fieldwork/      occupying the stations: cohort taxonomies and the
                    normal-versus-abnormal harmonization, stain normalization
                    and jitter, the multi-crop channels, slide-level splits.
    instruments/    the measuring gear: a self-distillation vision transformer
                    (ViT-S/16), its projection head, and the per-example
                    clip-and-noise that makes each reading differentially
                    private.
    triangulation/  fixing the network: the per-station drift (1 - cosine to
                    the consensus), the divergence-aware domain-structured
                    weights, the secure summation transport, the Renyi privacy
                    ledger, and the round-by-round traverse.
    adjustment/     the least-squares adjustment of the readings: the DINO
                    self-distillation objective.
    control/        the control marks that verify the survey: macro-F1,
                    balanced accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, AUC, ECE and
                    Brier, temperature rescaling, DeLong variance, bootstrap
                    intervals, Holm-Bonferroni and Benjamini-Hochberg,
                    Cochran's Q and I-squared, and the frozen-encoder linear
                    probe.
    datum/          the reference frame: seeding, atomic checkpoints, the gin
                    binding layer, and the typed station registers.
    sheets/         the working sheets you run: pretrain, probe, evaluate,
                    export.

  plans/            gin survey plans. principal.gin carries the reported
                    configuration; ablation_*.gin and supplementary_*.gin
                    redraw single dimensions; _smoke.gin is for the test bench
                    only and must not be used for any reported reading.

  control_points.txt  the verified cohort sources. Read this before laying
                      out any data.


STATION 1 — LAYING OUT THE INSTRUMENTS (install)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The toolchain is pinned hard, because a survey adjusted under one set of
  numerics must be reproducible under exactly the same ones. Expect a CUDA
  12.1 / PyTorch 2.1 / Python 3.9 environment and nothing more recent.

  pip:
      python3.9 -m venv .venv
      . .venv/bin/activate
      pip install -r requirements.txt
      pip install -e .

  conda:
      conda env create -f environment.yml
      conda activate schemafree

  docker:
      docker build -t schemafree .
      docker run --gpus all schemafree plans/principal.gin


STATION 2 — OCCUPYING THE STATIONS (data)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  control_points.txt holds the verified sources. Four cohorts are clients
  (each carries a few labels in its own taxonomy); two larger collections
  enter the unlabeled pretraining pool only.

    herlev         Denmark   conventional smear   7-class dysplasia
    sipakmed       Greece    conventional smear   5-class morphological
    mendeley_lbc   India     liquid-based         4-class Bethesda   CC BY 4.0
    cric           Brazil    conventional smear   6-class Bethesda

  The Brazilian station did not answer when this book was written and the
  identifier in circulation pointed elsewhere; cric stays the fourth client
  in the configuration but you must occupy its station from the maintained
  CRIC platform before its loader will read anything.

  Lay one image folder per cohort under a data root, then check the marks:

      scripts/fetch_marks.sh data

  With no data root present every sheet falls back to a planted-signal
  synthetic field, which is what the test bench and a dry run use.


STATION 3 — THE TRAVERSE (federated pretraining)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Each round, every party runs three local epochs of self-distillation under
  per-example clipping and Gaussian noise, reports an encoder update, and the
  server adjusts the network with the divergence-aware weights. Stain and
  scanner structure in the disagreement is damped; each party's normalization
  is kept local. No image and no label crosses a station boundary.

      python -m schemafree.sheets.pretrain plans/principal.gin --data-root data

  Multi-GPU, four ways:

      scripts/lay_baseline.sh plans/principal.gin data

  The principal plan fixes: ViT-S/16, 100 rounds, 3 local epochs, aggregation
  sharpness beta=2.0, projection dimension 512, student temperature 0.04,
  AdamW at 1e-3 on a cosine schedule, DP clip 1.0 with delta 1e-5 and Poisson
  rate 0.01, noise multiplier 0.62 at the epsilon=8 operating point.


STATION 4 — NAMING AND CHECKING (probe and evaluation)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Freeze the encoder, fit a linear head on a held-out cohort's few labels in
  its own taxonomy, then read the cross-cohort marks on the binary
  normal-versus-abnormal axis:

      scripts/adjust_network.sh plans/principal.gin runs/principal/encoder.ckpt

  Expected control marks on the leave-one-cohort-out axis, mean over 20 seeds:

      cross-cohort macro-F1   0.691 +/- 0.044
        herlev                0.625
        sipakmed              0.748
        mendeley_lbc          0.689
        cric                  0.701

  Sensitivity holds between 0.70 and 0.81 per cohort; AUC between 0.81 and
  0.87; expected calibration error 0.058 to 0.094 before a single temperature
  rescaling and roughly half that after. The centralized pooled bound sits
  above every federated entry, as it must; SchemaFree leads the deployable
  field on every cohort and sits below that bound.


STATION 5 — THE LEDGER (compute budget, stated plainly)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  4x NVIDIA A100 40GB, CUDA 12.1, PyTorch 2.1.
  Pretraining ~46.8 GPU-hours; downstream adaptation ~1.5 GPU-hours.
  Communication ~104 MB per round, about 18% above plain aggregation owing to
  secure summation. Total across every reported reading ~3,900 GPU-hours.
  These numbers are not negotiable down; budget for them before you start.


STATION 6 — EXPORT
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      python -m schemafree.sheets.export plans/principal.gin \
          --checkpoint runs/principal/encoder.ckpt --out runs/encoder.onnx


THE TEST BENCH
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      pytest -q
      ruff check . && black --check . && isort --check . && mypy src/schemafree

  The bench mixes property, regression, numerical, shape, overfit, and
  integration checks; the closed-form aggregation optimum, the privacy ledger,
  and the metric library are each pinned to an independent reference.
==============================================================================
